WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:02.530
Carly Fox: "Pregnant women
can't live with cats."

00:00:02.530 --> 00:00:05.560
This is a huge myth.

00:00:05.560 --> 00:00:08.180
Please don't get rid of your
cat if you are pregnant.

00:00:08.180 --> 00:00:11.540
"When cats purr, it means they're happy."

00:00:11.540 --> 00:00:13.240
This is definitely a myth.

00:00:13.240 --> 00:00:15.803
Ann Hohenhaus: "Cats think
their owner is their mother."

00:00:17.010 --> 00:00:18.841
Fox: Obviously your cat doesn't

00:00:18.841 --> 00:00:20.640
think that you're its mother.

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:22.050
I'm Dr. Carly Fox.

00:00:22.050 --> 00:00:24.830
I'm an emergency and
critical-care veterinarian

00:00:24.830 --> 00:00:27.210
at the Animal Medical
Center in New York City.

00:00:27.210 --> 00:00:28.720
Hohenhaus: And I'm Dr. Ann Hohenhaus,

00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:30.610
also at the Animal Medical Center,

00:00:30.610 --> 00:00:33.490
but I'm an internal medicine
and oncology specialist.

00:00:33.490 --> 00:00:35.769
Fox: Today we're going
to debunk some cat myths.

00:00:35.769 --> 00:00:36.743
[meow]

00:00:36.743 --> 00:00:37.890
"Cats love milk."

00:00:37.890 --> 00:00:40.300
I mean, I think this is sort of an image

00:00:40.300 --> 00:00:42.960
that has been put forth
throughout our childhood,

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:46.110
like, in storybooks and
in movies and on TV,

00:00:46.110 --> 00:00:48.820
but unfortunately,
cats, as they get older,

00:00:48.820 --> 00:00:50.450
actually are lactose intolerant.

00:00:50.450 --> 00:00:52.960
So their bodies actually
can't even digest milk.

00:00:52.960 --> 00:00:55.840
As kittens, they have an
enzyme called lactase,

00:00:55.840 --> 00:00:57.348
which helps them break down milk,

00:00:57.348 --> 00:00:59.897
because they are supposed to be drinking

00:00:59.897 --> 00:01:00.892
their mother's milk.

00:01:00.892 --> 00:01:03.659
But as they get older, that enzyme,

00:01:03.659 --> 00:01:07.050
which is usually very present, goes away.

00:01:07.050 --> 00:01:08.812
And then they're unable to digest milk.

00:01:08.812 --> 00:01:11.520
So if you feed milk to an older cat,

00:01:11.520 --> 00:01:12.930
or really any cat after they've

00:01:12.930 --> 00:01:14.420
been weaned from their mother,

00:01:14.420 --> 00:01:16.070
they really can't digest it.

00:01:16.070 --> 00:01:17.053
Even though they seem like

00:01:17.053 --> 00:01:18.580
they're really enjoying themselves,

00:01:18.580 --> 00:01:20.740
it actually can cause
gastrointestinal upset.

00:01:20.740 --> 00:01:22.270
Hohenhaus: You can go to
the pet store, though,

00:01:22.270 --> 00:01:23.411
and buy cat milk.

00:01:23.411 --> 00:01:24.244
[laughs]

00:01:24.244 --> 00:01:26.240
And the cat milk has two things

00:01:26.240 --> 00:01:27.840
that make it special for cats.

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:29.306
One is it's lactose-free,

00:01:29.306 --> 00:01:30.731
just like the lactose-free milk

00:01:30.731 --> 00:01:32.420
you can buy in the grocery store.

00:01:32.420 --> 00:01:35.320
But it also has extra taurine added to it.

00:01:35.320 --> 00:01:38.120
And cats require taurine in their diet.

00:01:38.120 --> 00:01:39.530
So it's just an extra source

00:01:39.530 --> 00:01:42.300
of that amino acid special for cats.

00:01:42.300 --> 00:01:43.390
Fox: I mean, who knew?

00:01:43.390 --> 00:01:45.110
Hohenhaus: "Cats are nocturnal."

00:01:45.110 --> 00:01:46.626
How can you be nocturnal when you

00:01:46.626 --> 00:01:48.209
sleep 23 hours a day?

00:01:48.209 --> 00:01:49.042
[laughs]

00:01:49.042 --> 00:01:51.570
The typical cat sleeps 23 hours a day.

00:01:51.570 --> 00:01:55.205
They wake up long enough
to kill some prey,

00:01:55.205 --> 00:01:57.150
eat that prey, and then go back to sleep

00:01:57.150 --> 00:01:58.600
until the next time they're hungry.

00:01:58.600 --> 00:02:01.040
Fox: They're actually crepuscular animals.

00:02:01.040 --> 00:02:03.281
Hohenhaus: They're what?
Fox: Crepuscular.

00:02:03.281 --> 00:02:04.610
That means that they're active

00:02:04.610 --> 00:02:06.235
during dusk and dawn,

00:02:06.235 --> 00:02:07.820
which goes back to what you just said

00:02:07.820 --> 00:02:10.360
about them hunting. So,
that's how lions hunt.

00:02:10.360 --> 00:02:12.240
They hunt in the dusk or the dawn,

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:13.890
where they can, you know,

00:02:13.890 --> 00:02:16.900
see prey better, hunt, and kill,

00:02:16.900 --> 00:02:19.980
and our domestic cats
actually evolved from that.

00:02:19.980 --> 00:02:21.500
So they're actually supposed to be

00:02:21.500 --> 00:02:24.230
most active in the morning
and in the evening,

00:02:24.230 --> 00:02:26.340
but not necessarily in
the middle of the night.

00:02:26.340 --> 00:02:27.900
Though some cats obviously are.

00:02:27.900 --> 00:02:29.730
Hohenhaus: Well, and they are
most active in the morning.

00:02:29.730 --> 00:02:31.902
Ask any cat owner. At 4
o'clock in the morning,

00:02:31.902 --> 00:02:33.468
that cat's walking on your head

00:02:33.468 --> 00:02:35.593
and running over the bed,
trying to get you up,

00:02:35.593 --> 00:02:37.210
because they don't have
to hunt for breakfast.

00:02:37.210 --> 00:02:38.800
They just have to get you up.

00:02:38.800 --> 00:02:41.601
Fox: "Cats hate water."

00:02:41.601 --> 00:02:43.860
Can't say that every cat hates water,

00:02:43.860 --> 00:02:45.820
but, I mean, in my experience,

00:02:45.820 --> 00:02:48.560
most cats definitely dislike water,

00:02:48.560 --> 00:02:51.470
as in they don't like
being bathed in water.

00:02:51.470 --> 00:02:53.060
You're definitely not gonna see most cats

00:02:53.060 --> 00:02:54.310
go for a swim.

00:02:54.310 --> 00:02:57.270
I'd say most cats don't love water,

00:02:57.270 --> 00:02:59.730
but when cats are feeling unkempt,

00:02:59.730 --> 00:03:02.350
perhaps they do like water.

00:03:02.350 --> 00:03:04.971
Hohenhaus: "Cats think their
owner is their mother."

00:03:06.589 --> 00:03:07.560
[laughs]

00:03:07.560 --> 00:03:08.868
I think that they just see you

00:03:08.868 --> 00:03:11.526
as a source of food and comfort

00:03:11.526 --> 00:03:14.010
and cleanliness and a safe place to live.

00:03:14.010 --> 00:03:15.863
Fox: Yeah, obviously your cat doesn't

00:03:15.863 --> 00:03:17.710
think that you're its mother,

00:03:17.710 --> 00:03:18.967
but they definitely think that you're

00:03:18.967 --> 00:03:20.850
its caretaker and they need you,

00:03:20.850 --> 00:03:22.791
but, you know,

00:03:22.791 --> 00:03:24.491
another person could
probably fill in that job

00:03:24.491 --> 00:03:26.580
just as easily for your cat, honestly,

00:03:26.580 --> 00:03:28.271
so I don't think that cats

00:03:28.271 --> 00:03:29.654
think that you're their mother.

00:03:29.654 --> 00:03:32.250
But some people definitely think that.

00:03:32.250 --> 00:03:34.800
"Pregnant women can't live with cats."

00:03:34.800 --> 00:03:37.810
This is a huge myth.

00:03:37.810 --> 00:03:40.520
Please don't get rid of your
cat if you are pregnant.

00:03:40.520 --> 00:03:44.190
Cats can sometimes be
infected with a parasite

00:03:44.190 --> 00:03:45.465
called toxoplasmosis,

00:03:45.465 --> 00:03:47.473
which can be shed in your cat's feces.

00:03:47.473 --> 00:03:50.000
If picked up by a pregnant woman,

00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:51.690
this parasite can sometimes

00:03:51.690 --> 00:03:53.430
cause birth defects or miscarriage,

00:03:53.430 --> 00:03:55.570
and that's obviously something
we would want to avoid.

00:03:55.570 --> 00:03:58.220
Cleaning the litter box
daily will help with this.

00:03:58.220 --> 00:03:59.694
You definitely don't want to

00:03:59.694 --> 00:04:02.110
leave the litter box to
go for more than one day

00:04:02.110 --> 00:04:04.070
because that can increase infection.

00:04:04.070 --> 00:04:05.440
If you do need to clean the litter box,

00:04:05.440 --> 00:04:06.410
you should just wear gloves.

00:04:06.410 --> 00:04:07.820
So the best thing that you can do

00:04:07.820 --> 00:04:10.290
is have someone clean
the litter box for you,

00:04:10.290 --> 00:04:11.810
which is also just great.

00:04:11.810 --> 00:04:13.080
Who wants to clean their litter box?

00:04:13.080 --> 00:04:14.800
It's a break for nine months.

00:04:14.800 --> 00:04:16.790
Hohenhaus: So, if you're
concerned about your health

00:04:16.790 --> 00:04:19.020
or your cat's health
during your pregnancy,

00:04:19.020 --> 00:04:20.978
be sure to bring up the topic with both

00:04:20.978 --> 00:04:23.830
your veterinarian and your obstetrician.

00:04:23.830 --> 00:04:26.160
"Cats can see in complete darkness."

00:04:26.160 --> 00:04:28.010
Cats have great night vision.

00:04:28.010 --> 00:04:31.720
They have, like, a mirror
in the back of their eye.

00:04:31.720 --> 00:04:34.640
And you know that from
taking photos of your cat

00:04:34.640 --> 00:04:36.756
because you see that yellow-green

00:04:36.756 --> 00:04:38.630
reflection in the camera,

00:04:38.630 --> 00:04:39.929
and that's this mirror

00:04:39.929 --> 00:04:41.546
that's in the back of the cat's eye

00:04:41.546 --> 00:04:43.500
that helps to reflect light around

00:04:43.500 --> 00:04:45.112
to improve their night vision.

00:04:45.112 --> 00:04:47.350
And that reflector area
is called the tapetum.

00:04:47.350 --> 00:04:49.630
Fox: Cats really can't
see in complete darkness.

00:04:49.630 --> 00:04:52.939
They still need a little
bit of light in their eye

00:04:52.939 --> 00:04:55.280
for it to bounce back
and forth within the eye

00:04:55.280 --> 00:04:57.440
off the tapetum, so complete darkness

00:04:57.440 --> 00:05:00.480
they cannot see in, but a lot of darkness

00:05:00.480 --> 00:05:01.650
with a little bit of light,

00:05:01.650 --> 00:05:03.800
they actually can see.

00:05:03.800 --> 00:05:06.342
Hohenhaus: "Human food is bad for cats."

00:05:06.342 --> 00:05:09.940
We don't recommend feeding
a human diet to cats,

00:05:09.940 --> 00:05:13.040
because it doesn't meet
their nutritional needs.

00:05:13.040 --> 00:05:14.760
Cats are obligate carnivores,

00:05:14.760 --> 00:05:16.940
and it means they need to eat meat.

00:05:16.940 --> 00:05:20.510
So your diet is not appropriate for cats.

00:05:20.510 --> 00:05:23.230
Fox: "Black cats are bad luck."

00:05:23.230 --> 00:05:25.890
I mean, this is obviously a huge myth.

00:05:25.890 --> 00:05:27.398
Hohenhaus: I think that black cats

00:05:27.398 --> 00:05:28.900
are bad luck for themselves,

00:05:28.900 --> 00:05:31.730
because they don't get
adopted from a shelter

00:05:31.730 --> 00:05:34.638
as readily as a pretty gray cat

00:05:34.638 --> 00:05:36.590
or a flashy tricolor cat.

00:05:36.590 --> 00:05:38.557
So the bad luck is actually for the cat,

00:05:38.557 --> 00:05:39.820
not for you.

00:05:39.820 --> 00:05:42.270
"Cats don't love people or babies."

00:05:42.270 --> 00:05:44.086
My mother was so worried about this

00:05:44.086 --> 00:05:47.010
when I was having a baby,
because I had these cats.

00:05:47.010 --> 00:05:48.787
And she said, "Those cats
are gonna climb in the crib

00:05:48.787 --> 00:05:50.480
and suffocate my grandson."

00:05:50.480 --> 00:05:51.950
Nothing like that happened.

00:05:51.950 --> 00:05:53.580
Babies are unpredictable,

00:05:53.580 --> 00:05:55.360
and they smell different than people,

00:05:55.360 --> 00:05:57.610
and they make different
movements than people,

00:05:57.610 --> 00:05:59.230
and they have stinky diapers.

00:05:59.230 --> 00:06:02.490
So I think this actually
might partly be true.

00:06:02.490 --> 00:06:03.940
It's not that they don't like babies.

00:06:03.940 --> 00:06:04.965
It's that they're different than

00:06:04.965 --> 00:06:06.610
the people they're used to.

00:06:06.610 --> 00:06:09.410
Fox: "Cats always land on their feet."

00:06:09.410 --> 00:06:12.760
Well, cats do have an
excellent righting reflex,

00:06:12.760 --> 00:06:14.590
meaning that a lot of the times

00:06:14.590 --> 00:06:16.440
they actually do land on their feet,

00:06:16.440 --> 00:06:18.160
and that has to do with their anatomy

00:06:18.160 --> 00:06:19.660
and their vestibular system.

00:06:19.660 --> 00:06:21.810
However, unfortunately,
I'm an emergency doctor,

00:06:21.810 --> 00:06:23.835
I live in New York City. I see many, many,

00:06:23.835 --> 00:06:25.560
many cats not land on their feet.

00:06:25.560 --> 00:06:26.860
Definitely don't think that your cat

00:06:26.860 --> 00:06:29.251
will just be fine if it unfortunately

00:06:29.251 --> 00:06:31.130
falls out of your third-story window

00:06:31.130 --> 00:06:33.430
or even from your top
of your refrigerator.

00:06:33.430 --> 00:06:34.979
Hohenhaus: And when they fall,

00:06:34.979 --> 00:06:36.412
they'll land on their chin,

00:06:36.412 --> 00:06:38.150
and they often fracture their wrists,

00:06:38.150 --> 00:06:40.830
and then if they belly flop,

00:06:40.830 --> 00:06:42.800
as opposed to land on their feet,

00:06:42.800 --> 00:06:45.808
they'll also get air in their lungs

00:06:45.808 --> 00:06:46.766
or around their lungs

00:06:46.766 --> 00:06:48.160
because their lungs get a little

00:06:48.160 --> 00:06:49.940
tear in it and start leaking.

00:06:49.940 --> 00:06:52.714
So these injuries are severe

00:06:52.714 --> 00:06:54.610
and life-threatening for cats.

00:06:54.610 --> 00:06:57.201
So the answer is get screens

00:06:57.201 --> 00:06:58.901
or don't open your windows.

00:06:58.901 --> 00:07:01.395
"Cats and dogs don't get along."

00:07:01.395 --> 00:07:03.540
I don't have any idea where
this would have come from.

00:07:03.540 --> 00:07:05.930
There are plenty of houses and households

00:07:05.930 --> 00:07:08.920
in the United States where
there are both dogs and cats

00:07:08.920 --> 00:07:10.460
and they're perfectly fine.

00:07:10.460 --> 00:07:12.510
Just like some people don't get along,

00:07:12.510 --> 00:07:14.500
sometimes a dog and cat don't get along,

00:07:14.500 --> 00:07:16.700
but sometimes you have two
dogs and they don't get along

00:07:16.700 --> 00:07:18.460
or two cats and they don't get along.

00:07:18.460 --> 00:07:21.710
So I think this is more
about the personality

00:07:21.710 --> 00:07:23.470
of your dog and your cat

00:07:23.470 --> 00:07:25.330
than it is that they can't get along.

00:07:25.330 --> 00:07:27.340
They're not gonna be the
next YouTube sensation,

00:07:27.340 --> 00:07:28.957
but I guess they

00:07:28.957 --> 00:07:31.280
maintain a working relationship.

00:07:31.280 --> 00:07:32.571
Hohenhaus: Yeah, yeah, that's good.

00:07:32.571 --> 00:07:33.787
A working relationship.

00:07:33.787 --> 00:07:35.730
We have to work together to be good pets.

00:07:35.730 --> 00:07:37.338
Fox: Yeah. Let's do that.

00:07:37.338 --> 00:07:39.855
"When cats purr, it means they're happy."

00:07:40.770 --> 00:07:42.300
This is definitely a myth,

00:07:42.300 --> 00:07:44.920
and I can tell you I've been scratched

00:07:44.920 --> 00:07:46.810
by many a purring cat.

00:07:46.810 --> 00:07:48.920
You know, I think purring is oftentimes

00:07:48.920 --> 00:07:50.570
associated with pleasure in cats;

00:07:50.570 --> 00:07:54.130
however, sometimes cats
can purr for other reasons,

00:07:54.130 --> 00:07:55.613
like they're very nervous,

00:07:55.613 --> 00:07:57.520
or it's a warning actually,

00:07:57.520 --> 00:07:58.770
or they're hungry,

00:07:58.770 --> 00:08:01.161
not necessarily that they're happy.

00:08:01.161 --> 00:08:04.590
"One human year equals seven cat years."

00:08:04.590 --> 00:08:07.280
This is definitely, definitely a myth.

00:08:07.280 --> 00:08:09.380
I think this is something
we more associate with dogs,

00:08:09.380 --> 00:08:12.110
but if you apply it to cats,

00:08:12.110 --> 00:08:14.530
I think it's even more of a myth.

00:08:14.530 --> 00:08:15.689
Hohenhaus: Well, and if
you look at it on the

00:08:15.689 --> 00:08:17.440
reverse end of the lifespan,

00:08:17.440 --> 00:08:19.800
a cat can have kittens
when it's 6 months old.

00:08:19.800 --> 00:08:23.360
6-month-old cat would be
3.5 years in human age,

00:08:23.360 --> 00:08:27.142
and clearly no 3.5-year-old children are

00:08:27.142 --> 00:08:29.263
having babies of their own.
Fox: I hope not.

00:08:29.263 --> 00:08:31.579
Hohenhaus: So, it
doesn't work in cats, no.

00:08:31.579 --> 00:08:33.490
Fox: Today we debunked some cat myths.

00:08:33.490 --> 00:08:35.367
There is a little bit of truth

00:08:35.367 --> 00:08:37.870
to some of these myths
that we talked about today,

00:08:37.870 --> 00:08:39.550
and I think that's very fitting,

00:08:39.550 --> 00:08:41.417
since cats are these very

00:08:41.417 --> 00:08:43.820
particular, special animals

00:08:43.820 --> 00:08:46.320
that are a bit of, like, a mixed bag,

00:08:46.320 --> 00:08:47.520
just like these myths.

00:08:47.520 --> 00:08:49.700
Hohenhaus: My son's
first words were "meow."

00:08:49.700 --> 00:08:50.533
[laughing]

00:08:50.533 --> 00:08:52.306
Producer: That's crazy.

00:08:52.306 --> 00:08:54.677
Hohenhaus: He would look
at the cat and go "meow."

00:08:54.677 --> 00:08:55.990
Fox: That's cute, really?

00:08:55.990 --> 00:08:57.497
[laughing]

